# Independent site build API review

Reviewed uncommitted feat/doc-site-build files tools/src/site/{mod,tests}.rs, tools/src/doc/canonical.rs, lib.rs, main.rs, site/{config.json,index.html,site.css}, production Doc CSS diff, and spec15. Read-only review. Root implements fixes. This is a docs-only composition/build review; missing publisher, Playground, full search/rustdoc/examples or S01–S06 implementation is not used as a finding against this bounded API.

## Open findings requiring correction

1. Source/tool identity does not establish the binary's origin. build() records current git HEAD and runs current rustc --version, while template/site CSS are compile-time include_str!/include_bytes! and production Doc assets/compiler are compiled into that binary. An older tools executable run against a newer clean checkout passes both git diff checks but emits its old embedded template/assets while build.json claims the new source commit and current rustc. Dirty checks also ignore untracked inputs, including a config or newly registered source. Spec15 requires fixed renderer/toolchain/input snapshot identities, not a label inferred from the execution directory. Record an actual executable/embedded-resource identity and truthful compiler provenance, verify embedded template/assets against selected source or snapshot them explicitly, and bind config/registry input bytes to the receipt. Add a small stale-template/binary mismatch regression rather than relying only on a clean Git worktree. Allowing an explicit separate renderer version is legitimate if its provenance is accurately distinguished from document source revision.

2. SiteConfig is not bounded before allocation/parsing. build() calls crate::json, whose read_to_string reads the entire file and whose JSON validator parses before SiteConfig.validate checks base.len(). A huge whitespace-padded otherwise valid config, or an oversized string, consumes arbitrary host memory/work before the 1024-byte base restriction can reject it. Use a bounded byte read and explicit total config limit before UTF-8/schema parsing. Test exact limit and limit+1, including whitespace padding, and leave output absent on rejection. repository::local_path already handles path confinement; do not replace that with an unchecked read.

3. MAX_SITE_BYTES excludes the completion manifest. compose() sums pages.files including build.json and checks 32 MiB, then serializes pages.manifest. write_generated writes that additional string as manifest.json, so a boundary artifact can exceed 32 MiB despite a successful size check. Include the actual final receipt bytes in checked output size (with checked arithmetic); exact/over-limit tests should count all disk payload, not just files indexed by the receipt. Also avoid presenting this late output-size check as protection against all intermediate allocation: links/identity vectors/JSON copies have already been built.

## Confirmed strengths and unaffected semantics

Canonical HTML generation is reused directly, and generated page bytes remain unchanged in compose(). canonical::html delegates to the same extracted generate_html implementation before writing; this refactoring does not replace the production parser/renderer with a second Markdown parser. SiteConfig rejects malformed bases, traversal, query/fragment/origin syntax, backslash and empty segments. Both /NEPL3/ and /acceptance/project/ are in tests. Page navigation requires each registry route to exist; escaping covers both href and ID label; insert rejects case-insensitive exact and file/directory collisions before mutation. Canonical generator already rejects its reserved manifest route collisions.

The fixed index uses no script, no inline style, and self-hosted stylesheet under the selected base; its default-src none/style-src self/base-uri none/form-action none CSP matches those resources. It labels Playground as under development and build.json records docs-only with null runtime_identity, avoiding fabricated runtime digests. Build/deploy receipts are distinct. Actual HTTP/CSP browser testing is not covered by the three unit tests.

Sentence correspondence is preserved by composition: it copies generated Doc HTML without regrouping paragraphs or flattening parallel variants. The CSS diff changes spacing, widths, headings/code/table styles and paragraph margin; it does not rewrite the nepl-parallel-rows/columns/single rules or sentence ownership. Existing inline-grid Ruby/Anno constraints remain; actual long sentence parallel rendering, 400% zoom/horizontal overflow and keyboard/read order need browser validation by the display reviewer. A clean visual impression alone does not prove correspondence semantics.

The config is loaded before source cleanliness is checked, canonical registry is read separately for navigation and generation, and current pre/post Git checks are not a substitute for binding exact immutable input bytes. No concurrent-change reproduction was run; do not overclaim this as a proven race exploit. The stale embedded-resource identity mismatch in finding 1 follows directly from compile-time inclusion versus runtime HEAD labeling.

## Independent validation and scope

Ran cargo test --locked -p nepl3-tools --lib site::tests: 3 passed, zero failed/ignored, 87 filtered. These verify base normalization, deterministic simple composition/digests and insert collisions. They do not exercise real build source provenance, input/output limits, production CSP or failure cleanup. No production-source edits or implementation probes were delegated. Approval is pending fixes and focused re-review of findings 1–3; broader T19/T20/runtime acceptance remains separately unverified.

# Re-review at checkpoint aee2935

Read final tools/src/site/mod.rs/tests.rs, tools/build.rs and doc/development.md. Original findings 1–3 are materially corrected: source_commit explicitly denotes input checkout; executable SHA-256 and compile-time rustc identify the renderer separately; no runtime PATH rustc is presented as compilation provenance. Three embedded template/CSS byte sequences are checked against current repository files. build.rs performs compiler identification only. An older renderer is no longer silently claimed to have been built from the input commit; source/binary association remains an external CI receipt responsibility, accurately documented.

Config now uses canonical::bounded with a 16 KiB total bound before UTF-8/duplicate-key/schema/base validation, regular-file and symlink checks, followed by tracked-file requirement. Final output-size checking includes manifest.json bytes and checked-add overflow. Independently ran all 5 site tests (5 passed; 87 filtered), including exact/over/overflow total-size cases and comparison of renderer identity with actual executable file SHA-256. Ran cargo build --locked -p nepl3-tools successfully.

Independent CLI probes in .tmp/site-review-probes/results.json: a valid whitespace-padded 16,384-byte config passed size/schema stages and failed only its intentionally untracked path; 16,385 bytes failed the input limit; duplicate key, unknown field and invalid UTF-8 each failed their correct boundary. All five returned exit 1 with no output directory. No tracked test fixtures or source were changed. These are real public CLI rejection probes, not simply assertions mirroring helper code.

Remaining input-provenance issue: git ls-files currently verifies only config. canonical registry/source/task reads otherwise rely on bounded reads and git diff --quiet HEAD. A committed clean registry can refer to an untracked Doc source supplied locally; Git's tracked diff remains clean and the source is accepted, although its bytes are not part of source_commit. Production doc-manifest carries source digests, which helps reproduce the actual input but does not establish that those inputs belong to the recorded checkout commit. Require all selected registry/tasks/Doc source/embedded asset inputs to be tracked, or explicitly distinguish/hash untracked input provenance in the site receipt. This follows directly from the public API path and Git's untracked-file behavior; a full expensive site-generation repro for this case was not run. Parent was notified; active production generation is not to be interrupted or its tracked inputs edited by this reviewer.

No new route/template/CSP or parallel-correspondence finding. Production page generation and browser/display checks remain root/other-reviewer work, not inferred from the five unit tests. Final approval is pending the remaining selected-input provenance decision/fix and scoped re-review. Prior initial review text above is retained as historical findings; it is superseded by this closure status.

# Selected-input provenance closure

Re-read the added tracking loop: doc/canonical.json, design/tasks.json, all three embedded-asset paths and every registry.pages source are now checked with git ls-files --error-unmatch before canonical generation, in addition to the config check. Aliases/projections are not read by this HTML generator; not requiring them here does not omit a generation input.

Independently reproduced the exact formerly uncovered condition without editing production or the shared checkout. Created a small Git repository under .tmp/site-untracked-review/fixture, committed config/registry/tasks and matching three assets, then supplied doc/untracked.nepld without adding it to Git. Confirmed git diff --quiet HEAD succeeds. Compiled a tiny harness linked to the actual freshly built nepl3-tools library and invoked the public site::build API against that repository. It rejected doc/untracked.nepld via git ls-files --error-unmatch before generation; output directory remains absent. Probe source and structured result are .tmp/site-untracked-review/probe.rs and result.json. This exercises the real API with a clean committed registry selecting an existing untracked source, rather than just reproducing a Git command in isolation.

Re-ran site tests after this fix: 5 passed, zero failed/ignored, 87 filtered. All review findings are now closed for the bounded docs-only build API. Source commit and executable identity are truthfully separate, selected inputs must be tracked, configuration reads and final output payload are bounded, and original route/CSP/correspondence findings remain unchanged (none). Approval is for this implementation scope, pending root's production artifact, alternate-base/browser/CI evidence; the earlier aee2935 production success predates this last tracking check and must not be relabeled as testing the new code.
